---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `let aName = "Avery";
let aScore = 88;
console.log(aName + ": " + aScore + " (" + (aScore >= 60 ? "pass" : "fail") + ")");

let bName = "Blake";
let bScore = 42;
console.log(bName + ": " + bScore + " (" + (bScore >= 60 ? "pass" : "fail") + ")");`;

const Figure2 = `function reportLine(name, score) {
  let verdict = "fail";
  if (score >= 60) {
    verdict = "pass";
  }
  console.log(name + ": " + score + " (" + verdict + ")");
}

reportLine("Avery", 88);
reportLine("Blake", 42);`;

const Figure3 = `// Before
console.log("Fuel: " + fuel + "%");
console.log("Oxygen: " + oxygen + "%");
console.log("Power: " + power + "%");

// After
function gauge(label, value) {
  console.log(label + ": " + value + "%");
}`;

const Task1 = `let aName = "Avery";
let aScore = 88;
let verdictA = "fail";
if (aScore >= 60) {
  verdictA = "pass";
}
console.log(aName + ": " + aScore + " (" + verdictA + ")");

let bName = "Blake";
let bScore = 42;
let verdictB = "fail";
if (bScore >= 60) {
  verdictB = "pass";
}
console.log(bName + ": " + bScore + " (" + verdictB + ")");
`;

const Task2 = `function reportLine(name, score) {
  let verdict = "fail";
  if (score >= 60) {
    verdict = "pass";
  }
  console.log(name + ": " + score + " (" + verdict + ")");
}

reportLine("Avery", 88);
reportLine("Blake", 42);
reportLine("Casey", 60);
`;

const Task3 = `function reportLine(name, score) {
  let verdict = "fail";
  if (score >= 60) {
    verdict = "pass";
  }
  console.log(name + ": " + score + " (" + verdict + ")");
}

reportLine("Avery", 88);
reportLine("Blake", 42);
reportLine("Casey", 60);

// Below is a block that repeats itself three times.
// Refactor it into one procedure and three calls.
console.log("Fuel: 82%");
console.log("  status: OK");
console.log("Oxygen: 91%");
console.log("  status: OK");
console.log("Power: 24%");
console.log("  status: LOW");
`;

const ExampleOutput = `Avery: 88 (pass)
Blake: 42 (fail)
Casey: 60 (pass)
Fuel: 82%
  status: OK
Oxygen: 91%
  status: OK
Power: 24%
  status: LOW`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.6: Refactoring</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.6</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Refactoring</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Changing Shape, Not Behavior</p>
              <p>Spotting the Repeat</p>
              <p>Test Before and After</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Definition</h2>
              <p><b>Refactoring</b> is changing how code is written without changing what it does.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Say That Again</h2>
              <p>Same inputs. Same outputs. Different shape.</p>
              <p class="fragment">If the behavior changed, you did not refactor. You edited.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Smell</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Two Blocks, One Idea" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">The giveaway is not the length. It is that the two blocks differ only in <b>values</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Move</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="One Procedure, Two Calls" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Whatever differed becomes a <b>parameter</b>. Whatever stayed the same becomes the body.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Recipe You Can Follow</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Find two blocks that differ only in values.</li>
                <li>Write down what differs. Those are your parameters.</li>
                <li>Move the shared code into a procedure.</li>
                <li>Replace both blocks with calls.</li>
                <li><b>Run it and compare the output to before.</b></li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>That Last Step</h2>
              <p>Refactoring is defined by the output not changing.</p>
              <p class="fragment">So the only way to know you refactored is to <b>check the output</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Copy the old output into a comment first. Compare after.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Not Always Worth It</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="A Small One" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Three one-line prints, into a procedure of one line.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Worth it if there will be twenty gauges. Not worth it if there will be three.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Judgment, not a rule.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining refactoring as changing structure without changing behavior</li>
                <li>Identifying repeated blocks that differ only in values</li>
                <li>Extracting a procedure and replacing the repeats with calls</li>
                <li>Verifying the output is unchanged</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Refactoring</dt>
                  <dd>Changing how code is written without changing what it does.</dd>
                  <dt>Extract a procedure</dt>
                  <dd>Move repeated code into a named procedure and call it.</dd>
                  <dt>Regression</dt>
                  <dd>A change that was supposed to be safe and was not.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-6-refactoring</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Record the Before</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li><b>Copy the exact output into a comment at the bottom.</b> You will compare against it.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Refactoring" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Extract the Procedure</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Replace the whole thing with the version below and run it.</li>
            <li>Compare against the comment from Task 1. The first two lines must match exactly.</li>
            <li>Casey scores exactly 60. Check the boundary is right.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Refactoring" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Do It Yourself</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Refactor the gauge block at the bottom into one procedure and three calls.</li>
            <li>The status line is indented by two spaces. Keep it that way.</li>
            <li>Run it and confirm the output is identical to before you started.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Refactoring" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Refactor a program you wrote in Quarter 1. Paste the before-output and after-output as comments and confirm they match character for character.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your program should produce output like this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">The point is not that this output is impressive. It is that it is <b>identical</b> to the version you started with.</p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Refactoring means behavior does not change. How did you actually verify that, rather than assume it?</li>
            <li>The gauge block had three repeats. At what number of repeats would you not have bothered? Defend the number.</li>
            <li>Task 2's Casey scores exactly 60. Why did that value get chosen for the example?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
